Q: How to configure refresh token with express and react A: <p>The cookie is <code>httpOnly</code>so you will not see the refresh token in the <strong><code>document.cookie</code></strong> that is expected and is the correct configuration for a refresh token. The important part is that your React app send credentials and Express must allow credentialed CORS.</p>
<h3><strong>Express</strong></h3>
<pre><code>import cors from &quot;cors&quot;;
import cookieParser from &quot;cookie-parser&quot;;

app.use(cors({
    origin: &quot;http://localhost:5173&quot;, // your React URL
    credentials: true
}));

app.use(cookieParser());
</code></pre>
<h3>Login request from React</h3>
<p>If you use <code>fetch</code> add <code>credentials: &quot;include&quot;</code>:</p>
<pre><code>await fetch(&quot;http://localhost:3000/login&quot;, {
    method: &quot;POST&quot;,
    headers: {
        &quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/json&quot;
    },
    credentials: &quot;include&quot;,
    body: JSON.stringify({
        email,
        password
    })
});
</code></pre>
<h3>Cookie</h3>
<p>For local HTTP development:</p>
<pre><code>res.cookie(&quot;refreshToken&quot;, refreshToken, {
    httpOnly: true,
    secure: false,
    sameSite: &quot;lax&quot;,
    maxAge: 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000,
    path: &quot;/&quot;
});
</code></pre>
<p>Then your refresh request must also include credentials</p>
<pre><code>await fetch(&quot;http://localhost:3000/refresh&quot;, {
    method: &quot;POST&quot;,
    credentials: &quot;include&quot;
});
</code></pre>
<p>Now Express can read it with</p>
<pre><code>const refreshToken = req.cookies.refreshToken;
</code></pre>
<p>Do <strong>not</strong> expect this to show the token because <code>httpOnly: true</code> deliberately prevents JavaScript from accessing the cookie.</p>
<pre><code>document.cookie
</code></pre>
<p>Instead you have to check Chrome DevTools - Application - Cookies and look under the backend domain.</p>
<p>So the key fix is</p>
<pre><code>// Express
credentials: true

// React
credentials: &quot;include&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Both are required when the React frontend and Express backend are different origins.</p>
<p>One correction to the original code I suggest because the database column is named <code>token_hash</code> the raw <code>refreshToken</code> should not normally be stored directly in that column. Hash it before saving and hash the incoming cookie before comparing it. That is separate from the cookie-not-being-received problem.</p>

Q: What&#39;s causing the empty space in this HTML/CSS? A: <p>It's most likely because you have used a mixture of absolute positioning and funky margining to move your boxes to where they should be.  Instead of doing that, I would nest them properly, it will be easier to maintain and you won't get into problems where they don't match up to where they should be</p>
